**This file cannot say what god's says.** god's NOTICE reads "No third-party
material is included in this work", and gog's lists two colour palettes. fathom
commits seventeen real JSON documents that other people made, because its
method requires them: `corpus/README.md` takes real files only, on the ground
that toy JSON is hard in ways nobody suffers from.

**The licence above does not, and cannot, re-license any of it.** Apache 2.0
covers fathom's own code and prose. Everything below keeps the terms it arrived
with, and a reader who wants to reuse one of these files answers to those terms
rather than to this repository's.


The corpus
----------

Seventeen documents under `corpus/<nn>-<name>/source.json`, each committed as a
specimen so that a measurement can be re-run against the exact bytes it was
made on. Two further entries — `04-gharchive` and `12-agent-trace` — ship a
`fetch.sh` instead, the first because it is 50 MB and the second because it is
a transcript of this project's own sessions.

Every entry's `NOTES.md` records the source URL and the day it was fetched.
That provenance is the authoritative record; this list is a summary.

  Public domain, open-government or explicitly open data

    03-natural-earth      Natural Earth 3.3.0 admin-0 countries. Public domain.
    09-stripe-openapi     Stripe's own OpenAPI 3 specification, from the
                          `stripe/openapi` repository.
    10-wikidata           Wikidata entity Q30. Wikidata's data is released CC0.
    13-package-lock       Visual Studio Code's `package-lock.json`.
    14-nyc-311            NYC Open Data, 311 service requests.
    18-openfda-events     openFDA adverse event reports, a US federal dataset.
    19-chicago-salaries   Chicago Open Data, city employee salaries.

  Made by a named author, under that author's terms

    05-fhir-bundle        Synthea, a SYNTHETIC patient bundle. No real patient
                          data is present, which is why this file could be
                          committed at all.
    11-jupyter-notebook   Peter Norvig, "Advent of Code 2021", from `pytudes`.
    16-movie-ratings      Rachael Tatman, "Data Cleaning Challenge", Kaggle
                          2018, with the accompanying notebook committed as
                          ground truth.
    17-openlibrary        OpenLibrary search results, Internet Archive.

  API responses, and the four that most warrant a reader's attention

    01-npm-registry       npm registry metadata for `express`, which carries
                          that package's own README text.
    02-hn-thread          336 Hacker News comments, via Algolia. **Each comment
                          was written by its author and is theirs.**
    06-espn-qbr           ESPN's NFL quarterback rating endpoint. Sports data,
                          supplied under ESPN's own terms.
    07-graphql-introspection  the SpaceX public GraphQL API describing its own
                          schema.
    08-open-meteo         Open-Meteo forecast data.
    15-github-issues      100 issues and pull requests from `pandas-dev/pandas`,
                          including text written by their authors.

**The last group is user-written or vendor-supplied content redistributed
verbatim**, and it is listed apart for that reason rather than because anything
is known to be wrong with it. They are held here as research specimens for a
tool comparison. If any rightsholder would rather a file were not committed,
`fetch.sh` is how the corpus already handles a document it cannot keep, and the
entry can move to that form without losing the measurement.


Runtime dependency
------------------

`flate2`, with the pure-Rust `miniz_oxide` backend, dual-licensed MIT and
Apache 2.0. It is the core's only runtime dependency; `Cargo.toml` says why a
decompressor is load-bearing where nothing else is.

Nothing else is linked. **The JSON parser is written here rather than taken**,
for the reasons in `design/implementation.md`: a conforming parser is entitled
to hide the duplicate keys, the `-0` and the bare `NaN` that the health verb
exists to report.


The book
--------

`book/apa.csl` is the APA 7th edition citation style from the Citation Style
Language project, **licensed CC BY-SA 3.0** and carrying that statement in its
own `<rights>` element. It is committed rather than fetched so that a build
cannot silently fall back to a different style.

It keeps its own licence, and it has to: **CC BY-SA forbids adding restrictions,
and the NonCommercial term on the book's prose is one.** So the file travels
under CC BY-SA 3.0 wherever this book goes. `book/LICENSE.md` says the same
thing about the book's two licences.


The test suite
--------------

`test/cases/` is generated and not committed; `test/generate.py` is the
artifact. Its truncation-ladder, NDJSON and "llm_trap" cases were adapted from
a JSON parser corpus built by another agent on 2026-08-08 and released CC0.
